{
  "summary": "The current build fails the primary user flow and has several accessibility and spec‑drift issues. The app never lands on the Sign‑In screen after launch (step 3 of the flow), causing a hard break. Axe reports an ARIA progressbar without an accessible name, making the loading UI invisible to screen‑readers. A review of the UI code shows missing or incomplete implementations of many required states (e.g., field‑level errors, loading overlays, empty‑state copy), inconsistent use of design tokens (hard‑coded colors/sizes appear in some components), lack of visible focus indicators, and missing accessibility markup such as proper headings, aria‑describedby links, and announcements for toasts. Navigation elements (bottom tab bar, drawer on web) are not guaranteed to be present on every signed‑in screen. These gaps mean the product is not yet polished or compliant with the DESIGN.md requirements.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "file": "expo-app/src/screens/SignInScreen.tsx",
      "issue": "The app never renders the Sign‑In screen after launch; the primary flow stops at step 3 with an empty page (no \"Welcome back\" title). This breaks the core onboarding flow.",
      "fix": "Update the SecureStore JWT check in the root navigator to correctly detect a missing token and push the Sign‑In screen. Ensure the SignInScreen renders a heading with the exact text \"Welcome back\" and that the navigation stack includes it as the default for unauthenticated users."
    },
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "file": "expo-app/src/components/LoadingOverlay.tsx",
      "issue": "Overlay spinners use a `<View role=\"progressbar\">` (or ActivityIndicator) without an accessible name, violating WCAG and causing the Axe \"ARIA progressbar nodes must have an accessible name\" error.",
      "fix": "Add `accessibilityRole=\"progressbar\"` and `accessibilityLabel=\"Loading…\"` (or the appropriate state‑specific label such as \"Creating account…\") to each loading indicator. Alternatively, use React‑Native‑Paper's `ActivityIndicator` with the `accessibilityLabel` prop."
    },
    {
      "severity": "high",
